  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Nebraska total $948M for FY2026 to date. Award activity shows 13,792 awards with an average award size of $68.7K. Obligations are down 25.2% year over year. Current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date), so the FY2026 figures represent spending through the current reporting period rather than a full fiscal year. Historical fiscal-year obligations ranged from $1.3B in FY21, FY22, FY24, and FY25, to $2B in FY23, giving context for the current partial-year total. The Department of Defense, Department of Education, and General Services Administration are the largest buyers so far, and NELNET SERVICING LLC and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. lead the vendor list. Major industry categories include Other Activities Related to Credit Intermediation and Computer Systems Design Services, which together account for $289M and $183M respectively. This snapshot highlights where federal dollars are concentrated in Nebraska during the FY2026 reporting period.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Nebraska spending changed this year compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Obligations are down 25.2% year over year, and the FY2026 total stands at $948M for the fiscal year to date. By comparison, recent full fiscal years recorded $1.3B in FY21, $1.3B in FY22, $2B in FY23, $1.3B in FY24, and $1.3B in FY25. The current FY2026 numbers are partial and may change as the fiscal year progresses."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries dominate Nebraska federal contracting right now?",
        "body": "The top buying agencies are the Department of Defense ($334M), the Department of Education ($289M), and the General Services Administration ($154M). The leading vendors are NELNET SERVICING LLC ($289M) and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. ($143M). Major industry categories include Other Activities Related to Credit Intermediation ($289M) and Computer Systems Design Services ($183M)."
      }
    ]
